        topic: "Mixed",
        fp: "A master commercial stone carver entered into a written contract with an art museum to sculpt a monumental white marble garden fountain for $80,000, payable in four progress installments of $20,000 as carving milestones were achieved, with completion scheduled for September 1. The contract contained the following clause: 'No assignment of rights or delegation of performance under this contract shall be made by either party without the prior written consent of the other; any assignment without consent shall be void.' On July 15, after completing the first three milestones and receiving $60,000 in progress payments, the stone carver suffered an acute cardiac episode requiring immediate double-bypass heart surgery. His physician ordered him to cease all heavy physical labor for at least one year. The carver immediately telephoned the museum director, explained his medical disability, and stated that he would be physically unable to complete the fourth milestone. The museum solicited bids from three licensed, reputable master stone masons to carve the final decorative friezes; the lowest competitive bid was $28,000. The museum hired the replacement mason, who completed the fountain on September 1 for $28,000. The original carver sued the museum in quantum meruit, seeking $10,000, arguing that the reasonable market value of his completed 75% labor and marble was $70,000, whereas he had been paid only $60,000.",
