        exp: "Rule: Under the landmark Pinkerton doctrine (Pinkerton v. United States), a conspirator is criminally liable for any substantive crime committed by a co-conspirator IF: (1) the crime was committed in furtherance of the common unlawful objective, and (2) the crime was a natural and probable consequence (foreseeable result) of the conspiracy. It is well-established that violent armed resistance to security or police apprehension during the commission of, or immediate escape from, a nighttime residential burglary is a natural and probable consequence of a criminal conspiracy. Private understandings or assumptions that weapons will not be used do not shield a co-conspirator from liability for foreseeable violence committed to effectuate an escape. Because the shooting occurred during immediate escape from the burglary, Defendant is liable for the attempted murder of the officer under Pinkerton (Option A). Option B is incorrect because Pinkerton liability imposes full vicarious liability for the target offense committed by the partner (attempted murder). Option C is incorrect because escape is legally part of the continuous transaction of the conspiracy, and armed resistance to arrest is foreseeable. Option D is incorrect because Pinkerton requires foreseeability and furtherance; it is not absolute strict liability for completely unrelated acts."
