        ans: 2,
        exp: "Rule: Under Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability § 2(b), a product is defective in design when the foreseeable risks of harm posed by the product could have been reduced or avoided by the adoption of a reasonable alternative design (RAD), and the omission renders the product not reasonably safe. In modern products liability, providing an instruction or warning—or supplying a detachable safety guard that can easily be removed—does NOT insulate a manufacturer from design liability if the removal or misuse is reasonably foreseeable, and an integrated safety design (such as a permanently fastened transparent cover or an automatic power-cutoff interlock switch) was technologically and economically feasible without destroying the product's primary utility. Audiophiles operating tube gear with cages removed to view the glowing tubes is a well-known, highly foreseeable consumer behavior. If an inexpensive integrated interlock was feasible, the design is defective (Option C). Option A is incorrect because warnings and detachable guards do not cure a defective design when a feasible, safer alternative is available. Option B is incorrect because strict products liability protects foreseeable bystanders. Option D is incorrect because manufacturers are held to strict products liability for defective designs, not absolute insurer liability."
