        fp: "A commercial developer entered into a written contract with an architectural firm to design a six-story luxury boutique hotel for $150,000, payable in installments as design phases were completed. The contract contained the following clause: 'This agreement constitutes the final, complete, and exclusive expression of the agreement between the parties, and supersedes all prior agreements, oral or written.' Before signing the written contract, the developer's managing partner orally stated to the architect: 'Our company has submitted an application to the city council for a commercial height variance; it is expressly understood that our agreement to proceed with these hotel designs is conditioned upon the city council granting that height variance by July 1.' The architect orally replied: 'Understood; if you don't get the variance, the deal is off.' The written agreement omitted any mention of the zoning variance or any conditions. The city council denied the height variance on June 15. The developer immediately notified the architect that the project was canceled. The architect completed the preliminary blueprints and sued the developer for $50,000, arguing that the Parol Evidence Rule barred all evidence of the prior oral understanding regarding the zoning variance.",
