        exp: "Rule: Under the rescue doctrine and emergency mitigation principles (Restatement (Third) of Torts § 32), an individual who acts in an emergency to protect human life (his infant daughter) or property from an imminent, life-threatening peril created by a defendant's tortious conduct (a defectively designed or manufactured fire-causing product) does NOT commit a superseding act or voluntarily assume the risk, provided the rescue effort is not completely reckless or rash under the emergency circumstances. Rushing to pick up and discard an active fire source threatening an entire household is a normal, foreseeable human emergency reaction. The rescue doctrine applies to products liability claims, preserving the manufacturer's proximate liability to the injured rescuer (Option C). Option A is incorrect because an emergency intervention to extinguish an active house fire is not voluntary assumption of the risk. Option B is incorrect because emergency reactions to product-caused fires are foreseeable, not superseding misuses. Option D is nonsensical."
